// THE BOUND ON D207's RUNG, AS A PROGRAM — the `-3` map-value floor with NO destination.
// **IT WAS PAID OFF ON PURPOSE (D461, 2026-08-28) AND NOW RUNS, printing `1`.**
//
// It was filed as a cell that must keep LOUD-REJECTING, and the reasoning was right about
// D207's rung and wrong about the row: `letMapDestShape` is a FIND, so admitting the `-3`
// floor to it could only land a binding on a slot that already existed, and here there is no
// destination and therefore no slot — `reach=1`, `ans=0`, measured. The cell's job was to
// flip the day someone made that rung MINT instead of find. It did not: D461 closed the floor
// one container UP, at the resolver that decides which struct row the value's shape names,
// and D207's rung is untouched and still a find.
//
// WHAT KEEPS IT IN THE SET, now as a `runs` cell. `structIndexOfObjCtx` adopts `{ r: 7 }` onto
// Circle's row through `anonValueFitsField`'s union-box leniency and that adoption SUPPRESSES
// the `#anon` row, so the shape the checker named has exactly one row and it is Circle's;
// `structIndexOfTypeName` refused that same pairing as a proven code mismatch and the render
// named nothing. The two resolvers now agree — the NAME one re-scans with the NODE one's
// leniency, but only for a render the strict pass placed nowhere. If this cell goes back to
// `emitProgram: unsupported map value type`, that second pass has been lost; if it starts
// answering something OTHER than `1`, the two resolvers have been made to agree on the wrong
// row, which is the failure mode the strict-pass-first ordering exists to prevent (running
// the leniency as ONE pass costs `tests/cases/maps/inferred-map-destination-shape.vl`,
// measured).
type Circle = { r: i32 | null }
const lv1 = Map()
lv1["k0"] = { r: 7 }
print(lv1.size)
